{
  "abstract": "The scaled reciprocal moment of a finite Syracuse residue law differs from the uniform harmonic comparator by a term governed by the weighted logarithmic mass of divergent Collatz components. At every fixed exponential cutoff the centered sum measures orbit occupation, using Valbuena's passage and component results. Tilted moments have an explicit transition at exponents $1+\\xi/n$. At exponent two the limit detects any divergent orbit, including one in a component of zero logarithmic density, and allows additional cycles. Total variation convergence of discounted stopping locations gives the ordinary-depth scalar limit at every fixed odd target. For the union of complete shortcut orbits from starts through $M$, the reciprocal-square masses surviving through $m$ and first entering at $m+1$ both have order $M^{-1}\\rho^m(m+1)^{-3/2}$ from a fixed clock through $k=\\lfloor\\log_2 M\\rfloor$, where $\\rho=2^{H_2(\\log_3 2)-1}$. The excess first-entry time is tight, with conditional survival $O(\\rho^s)$ for excess $s$ up to a constant times $(\\log k)^2/\\log\\log k$, even after adding a specified number of complete orbits. For survival above the floor alone, conditional source discrepancy and Spitzer's identity give the leading coefficient throughout that window as an explicit positive, nonconstant periodic function of $(k+s)\\log_3 2$. A separate source estimate gives a strict power gain at a fixed proportional extension of the dyadic horizon.",
